speechtext:
My hon. friend says, "That is right". I will deal with that in a minute;
I will deal with all the possibilities. My hon. friends seemingly are quite excited about it. We hear a great deal about hon. members, once a party policy is decided upon, supporting that policy. I think the Cooperative Commonwealth Federation have had something to say about that. I do not know any group in this house that stick more closely together as a party than the Cooperative Commonwealth Federation. The time is coming when they should stop talking like that, because they must look forward to the time-I hope they are not without hope among themselves-when they might form a government, and they never could run this country unless they stick to the policy of party solidarity. So they should stop decrying the only way in which a government can be run in this dominion.
There is a lot of talk about voting for a party policy once it is agreed upon. Supposing this government were to tell hon. members, "Vote as you please on this bill; we do not care whether you defeat it or not". I ask every hon. member to answer honestly to himself this question: If that were done, would there be any assurance that the price of wheat would be raised one 5-cent piece over 70 cents?
